25. FIREBASE SECURITY
==================================================

Create a production-oriented Firestore Security Rules file.

Create appropriate Cloud Storage Security Rules.

Apply least-privilege access.

Required security principles:

1. Customers may read and update only permitted fields of their own profiles.
2. Customers cannot grant themselves seller or administrator roles.
3. Sellers can modify only their own shops and products.
4. Seller access is conditional on approval.
5. Customers cannot alter another customer's orders.
6. Order creation must validate ownership and permitted fields.
7. Order totals and payment states must not be trusted when submitted by clients.
8. Reviews must follow the defined review policy.
9. Users may read only notifications they are authorized to access.
10. Sensitive payment information must not be exposed to unauthorized clients.
11. Audit logs must not be editable by ordinary users.
12. Public product and shop reads must respect publication and moderation status.
13. Storage uploads must enforce reasonable size and file-type restrictions.
14. Administrative actions must use trusted authorization.
15. Deny access by default when no explicit rule permits it.

Where possible, restrict updates to specific allowed fields.

Remember that Firebase Admin SDK operations bypass Firestore Security Rules, so trusted backend functions must enforce their own authorization and validation.

Create automated tests for critical Security Rules.

Do not leave the database publicly writable.

Do not use insecure rules such as allowing every authenticated user to read and write every document.

